WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.692
Cleaning historical data is useful, but preventing future problems is even better.

00:00:05.692 --> 00:00:11.222
Once a rule is stable and well understood, the database can help enforce it.

00:00:11.222 --> 00:00:14.657
A not-null constraint can require a value.

00:00:14.657 --> 00:00:18.982
A check constraint can enforce a domain or pattern rule.

00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:25.062
A unique constraint can prevent duplicate values when uniqueness is truly required.

00:00:25.062 --> 00:00:28.934
A foreign key protects relationships between tables.

00:00:29.591 --> 00:00:37.211
For example, if every order must reference an existing customer, a foreign key can prevent an order from pointing to a

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:39.901
customer identifier that does not exist.

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:43.776
Constraints should represent rules we are confident about.

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:46.806
Do not use them to encode guesses.

00:00:46.806 --> 00:00:51.171
When a business rule is unclear, investigate first.

00:00:51.171 --> 00:00:56.613
When the rule becomes stable, move it closer to the point where data enters the system.
